"""Report content: chapters 3 and 4."""

CH3 = [
    ("chapter", 3, "MATHEMATICAL MODELING & THEORETICAL FORMULATIONS"),
    ("intro", "This chapter defines the signal model, the query-conditioned network, the training objective, absent-query training, the detector, the competitive mask and the evaluation metrics. Every quantity defined here is used in the implementation described in Chapter 4, and the constants are the ones used in the released code."),
    ("h2", "3.1 Signal Model and Time-Frequency Representation"),
    ("p", "A mixture x(t) is the sum of K source signals s_{k}(t), where k indexes the instruments present in the recording."),
    ("eq", "eq_mix", "x(t) = ∑_{k=1}^{K} s_{k}(t)"),
    ("p", "The separator works on the short-time Fourier transform (STFT) of the mixture. At a sample rate of 22,050 Hz it uses a Hann window w(n) of N = 1024 samples and a hop of H = 256 samples, which gives F = 513 frequency bins. A 6 s clip contains 517 frames."),
    ("eq", "eq_stft", "X(f, τ) = ∑_{n=0}^{N−1} w(n) · x(n + τH) · e^{−j2πfn/N}"),
    ("p", "The network predicts a real mask M with values in [0, 1] for every time-frequency bin. The estimate keeps the phase of the mixture and replaces the magnitude with the masked one."),
    ("eq", "eq_recon", "ŝ_{q} = iSTFT( M_{q} ⊙ |X| ⊙ e^{j∠X} )"),
    ("p", "Reusing the mixture phase is a deliberate simplification. It limits the quality that a mask can reach, and an oracle reference is measured in Chapter 5 with an ideal ratio mask."),
    ("h2", "3.2 Query-Conditioned Masking with FiLM"),
    ("p", "The query q is an integer from 0 to 7 that names a stem. A learned embedding table E maps it to a vector e_{q} of 64 dimensions. Raw STFT magnitudes span about five orders of magnitude, so quiet bins, where hiss and bleed live, are nearly invisible to the first convolution. The magnitude is therefore log-compressed before it enters the encoder, while the mask is still applied to the raw magnitude."),
    ("eq", "eq_log", "X̃ = log( 1 + 10 · |X| )"),
    ("p", "At every encoder level l the feature maps h_{l} with C_{l} channels are modulated by a scale and a shift that a linear layer computes from the query embedding. This is feature-wise linear modulation (FiLM) [24]."),
    ("eq", "eq_film", "h'_{l,c} = γ_{l,c}(e_{q}) · h_{l,c} + β_{l,c}(e_{q})"),
    ("p", "Four encoder blocks (4x4 convolutions with stride 2, batch normalisation and LeakyReLU) have 64, 128, 256 and 512 channels. A 3x3 bottleneck follows. Four decoder blocks with transposed convolutions restore the resolution, and skip connections concatenate the encoder features of the matching level. A 1x1 convolution and a sigmoid produce the mask. Figure {F:unet} shows the structure."),
    ("eq", "eq_mask", "M_{q} = σ( f_{θ}(X̃, e_{q}) )"),
    ("fig", "unet", "fig_3_1_unet_film.png", 14.6, "Figure {N}: Query-Conditioned U-Net with FiLM Modulation at Every Encoder Level"),
    ("h2", "3.3 Training Objective"),
    ("p", "Let S be the magnitude STFT of the target stem, let x̄ be the mean of |X| over the clip, and let ε be a small constant (10^{-6} added to x̄, 10^{-8} in the ratio mask). Five terms are combined. The first compares the masked magnitude with the target, normalised by the loudness of the mixture so that the term has no absolute scale."),
    ("eq", "eq_rel", "L_{rel} = mean( | M · |X| − S | ) / ( x̄ + ε )"),
    ("p", "The second term works on a log-compressed magnitude. It penalises residual noise in quiet bins, which the first term barely sees."),
    ("eq", "eq_logl", "L_{log} = mean | log(1 + 100 · M · |X|) − log(1 + 100 · S) |"),
    ("p", "The third term is the squared error to the ideal ratio mask, and the fourth penalises mask energy in bins where the mixture contains energy that does not belong to the target."),
    ("eq", "eq_irm", "L_{irm} = mean[ ( M − clip( S / (|X| + ε), 0, 1 ) )² ]"),
    ("eq", "eq_int", "L_{int} = mean( M ⊙ ReLU(|X| − S) ) / ( x̄ + ε )"),
    ("p", "The last term works on the waveform. SI-SDR in decibels is clipped to [−30, 30] and negated, and it is applied only when the target is not silent, because SI-SDR is undefined for an empty reference."),
    ("eq", "eq_sdr", "L_{sdr} = − clip( SI-SDR(ŝ, s), −30, 30 )"),
    ("eq", "eq_total", "L = L_{rel} + 0.5 L_{log} + 0.5 L_{irm} + 0.2 L_{int} + 0.05 L_{sdr}"),
    ("p", "The small weight on L_{sdr} matters. An earlier version gave the SI-SDR term a weight of 0.1 per decibel. That term was about a hundred times larger than the L1 term, and because SI-SDR ignores scale, the mask drifted to arbitrary gains and a hissy noise floor. With the scale-free terms above and a weight of 0.05, the waveform term applies pressure without taking over."),
    ("h2", "3.4 Absent-Query Training"),
    ("p", "With probability p = 0.2, a training sample asks for a stem that is not in the clip, for example a mridangam on a Western pop excerpt, and its target is set to zero. When S = 0, the terms L_{rel}, L_{log}, L_{int} and L_{irm} all reduce to penalties on the mask itself, and L_{sdr} is switched off. The only minimum of the remaining loss is a mask close to zero."),
    ("eq", "eq_absent", "S = 0  ⇒  L = L_{rel} + 0.5 L_{log} + 0.5 L_{irm} + 0.2 L_{int}  ⇒  M → 0"),
    ("p", "The consequence at inference time is that a request for an instrument that is not in the song returns near-silence and not a copy of the mixture or a layer of noise. Sato et al. [5] describe the same practice for sound extraction and report that it costs about 0.9 dB on matched queries, a trade-off to keep in mind in Chapter 5."),
    ("h2", "3.5 Stem Detector Formulation"),
    ("p", "The detector network maps a log-mel spectrogram of a 6 s window to eight logits z_{k}, one per stem, and six of the eight are trained. Training uses a binary cross-entropy that is weighted per class and masked per label. Neither corpus has separate guitar and piano ground truth, so those two labels have m_{k} = 0, receive no gradient and are not used at inference."),
    ("eq", "eq_bce", "L_{det} = ∑_{k} m_{k} · w_{k} · BCE(z_{k}, y_{k}) / ∑_{k} m_{k}"),
    ("p", "The class weights w_{k} are 1 for the Western stems, 1.5 for violin and mridangam and 2 for ghatam, to offset their lower frequency in the data. At inference the logits of up to ten windows spread over the song are averaged, and each class has a tuned threshold τ_{k} that is selected on validation data to maximise F1. The logit is shifted so that its tuned threshold maps to probability 0.5."),
    ("eq", "eq_det", "p_{k} = σ( z̄_{k} − logit(τ_{k}) ),   stem k is reported if p_{k} ≥ 0.5"),
    ("h2", "3.6 Competitive Mask Filtering"),
    ("p", "Running each stem through its own mask lets bleed through, because nothing forces the masks of different stems to share a bin. The final system treats the bin as a quantity that is divided among all sources. Let A_{s}(f, τ) be the magnitude of source s in the 44.1 kHz STFT (n_fft 2048, hop 512), whose frame grid equals the 22.05 kHz grid used by the U-Net. Let c be the total mask claimed by the U-Net stems, clipped to [0, 1] so that the remaining share 1 − c cannot become negative, and let p = 2. Each source gets a weight, and the output keeps the share of the selected stems."),
    ("eq", "eq_c", "c = clip( M_{violin} + M_{mridangam} + M_{ghatam}, 0, 1 )"),
    ("eq", "eq_w", "W_{s} = ( A_{s} · (1 − c) )^{p},   W_{u} = ( M_{u} · |X| )^{p}"),
    ("eq", "eq_share", "Y = ∑_{s ∈ S_{sel}} S_{s} ⊙ W_{s} / ( ∑_{j} W_{j} + ε )"),
    ("p", "In Equation ({E:share}) the sum over j runs over all competing sources, drums, bass, vocals, guitar and piano from the Demucs-6 model and violin, mridangam and ghatam from the U-Net, and S_{sel} is the set of stems the user selected. The \"other\" output of Demucs competes only for the U-Net stems, because it holds real content such as synthesisers and would otherwise take energy that belongs to guitar and piano. Figure {F:compete} illustrates the idea."),
    ("p", "A last step restores loudness. If the peak of the output is above 0.95 it is scaled to 0.92. If the peak is at least 0.03, a gain of min(0.89 / peak, 6) is applied. An output below that peak is treated as an absent stem and left alone, so that residue is not amplified into noise."),
    ("fig", "compete", "fig_3_2_competitive_mask.png", 14.8, "Figure {N}: Competitive Mask. Each Time-Frequency Bin Is Shared by All Sources and the Selected Stem Keeps Only Its Share"),
    ("h2", "3.7 Evaluation Metrics"),
    ("p", "Separation quality is reported as scale-invariant SDR [25]. The estimate is projected on the reference to remove any difference in gain."),
    ("eq", "eq_sisdr", "SI-SDR = 10 · log_{10}( ‖αs‖² / ‖αs − ŝ‖² ),   α = ⟨ŝ, s⟩ / ‖s‖²"),
    ("p", "Leakage is reported as the source-to-interference ratio (SIR). The estimate is regressed on the ground-truth stems of the track by least squares, and SIR is the energy of the part explained by the target stem over the energy of the part explained by the other stems. A higher value means less bleed."),
    ("eq", "eq_sir", "SIR = 10 · log_{10}( ‖c_{target}‖² / ‖∑_{j ≠ target} c_{j}‖² ),   ŝ ≈ ∑_{j} c_{j}"),
    ("p", "Two reference values put these numbers in context. The raw mixture used as the estimate is the trivial baseline. The ideal ratio mask, computed from the true stem and applied to the mixture while keeping the mixture phase, is an oracle reference. It is not a strict upper bound for every possible mask model. The detector is scored with F1 per class and the macro average over the six classes that have ground truth."),
]

