    ("h2", "4.3 Stem Detector Implementation"),
    ("p", "The detector (models/stem_detector.py) is a small convolutional network of 0.42 million parameters. It takes a 128-band log-mel spectrogram, passes it through four blocks of convolution, batch normalisation, ReLU and max-pooling with 32, 64, 128 and 256 channels, applies global average pooling, and ends with two fully connected layers that output eight logits. Six of the eight outputs are trained. The other two, guitar and piano, are never trained and are not used. Figure {F:det} shows the layers."),
    ("fig", "det", "fig_4_3_detector.png", 14.8, "Figure {N}: Stem Detector Architecture"),
    ("p", "Two choices in training address a problem found early in the project: a detector trained only on the real corpora learned the dataset and not the instrument, because every MUSDB clip had Western instruments and every Saraga clip had Carnatic ones. The first choice is synthetic cross-corpus mixing. With probability 0.6 a training sample is a sum of clean stem clips drawn from both corpora with random gains between −8 and +3 dB, labelled by the stems that went in. The second is the label mask described in Section 3.5. Training used AdamW with a learning rate of 1e-3, weight decay 1e-4, a cosine schedule, gradient clipping at 5.0 and 40 epochs with a batch of 16. The checkpoint with the best validation macro-F1 was kept, which was epoch 31 with a macro-F1 of 0.798."),
    ("p", "At inference (inference/pipeline.py, detect_stems) the network scores between 3 and 10 windows spread across the song and averages the logits. The Demucs energy shares then check the result. Vocals, drums and bass are fused as 0.7 times the detector probability plus 0.3 times a scaled energy share, and a stem whose energy share is below 0.035 is vetoed. Violin, mridangam and ghatam use the detector alone, because Demucs has no such classes. The guitar and piano outputs of the detector were never trained, so their probabilities come from the energy share of the guitar and piano outputs of the 6-source model. If the detector recognises Carnatic audio (violin, mridangam or ghatam at 0.5 or above and drums below 0.5), the guitar probability is capped at 0.2."),
